    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d561">

                <pb facs="0829" n="723" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg723"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">361.1115 Kujala, Arthur J./7:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1938-11-14T15:00:00+03:00">November 14,
                            1938—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 14—11:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>388. Department’s instruction No. 408 of July 11 this year.<anchor n="52"
                        xml:id="d561-f1"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>According to a note received last evening from the Foreign Office
                            Arthur Kujala was arrested in 1938<anchor n="53"
                            xml:id="d561-f2"/> “for reprehensible actions”<anchor n="54"
                            xml:id="d561-f3"/> and at that time he possessed neither documentary
                        evidence of foreign citizenship nor a Soviet residence visa. The note adds
                        that “Kujala was sentenced to 5 years in a reformatory labor camp and is now
                        serving his term of detention”.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>The Embassy is making appropriate inquiry regarding the specific charges
                        under which the [<hi rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] Kujala was arrested and
                        tried, his <pb facs="0830" n="724" xml:id="pg724"/> present whereabouts and
                        the earliest possible date on which he may be interviewed by a member of the
                        staff.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Should the Department so instruct, and in case his relatives are willing
                        and able to make available $100 for his repatriation, the Embassy will
                        endeavor to have Kujala deported.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d561-f1" n="52" xml:id="d561-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d561-f2" n="53" xml:id="d561-f2-content">
                <p>The arrest occurred on September 23, 1937.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d561-f3" n="54" xml:id="d561-f3-content">
                <p>In his deposition at the American Legation in Helsinki on September 13, 1939,
                    after his deportation from the Soviet Union, Kujala declared that the only
                    reason for his arrest was “I didn’t have a passport.” (361.1115
                        Kujala, Arthur J./38)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
